

[38;5;231m⏺[39m That second answer changes the classification. "Persist and work across the app — other forms will need it later"
  describes a new subsystem: storage with a lifetime, plus a shared interface that components which don't exist yet will
  consume. That's not a one-file change to an existing flow, however small the first edit in [38;5;153mapp.js[39m looks.

  [1mRe-classifying as architectural.[0m The ratchet is one-way, so I'm stepping up to the full process: clarifying questions
  one at a time, then 2-3 approaches with trade-offs, then a sectioned design for your approval, then a written spec at
  [38;5;153mdocs/hyperpowers/specs/[39m before any code. No code until you've approved that spec. Your first answer carries over —
  [38;5;153mlogin()[39m returns the userId rather than taking it — that's now the subsystem's entry point rather than the whole
  change.

  Nothing else I do depends on the next answer, so here's the first question.

  [1mThe fork:[0m "track who logged in, persisting across the app" splits into two different data models, and they're
  expensive to swap later because the consuming forms get built against whichever one we pick.

  - [1mCurrent-user store[0m — one live value: who is logged in right now. Other forms read it to stamp submissions or gate
    UI. Cheap, small interface, single source of truth. Rules out answering "when did they log in" or "what did they do"
    without adding a second mechanism.
  - [1mEvent log[0m — an append-only list of tracked events, login being the first kind. Answers history questions and extends
    to other forms' actions naturally. Costs an event schema, a size/retention policy, and more surface area. "Who is
    logged in now" becomes a derived query rather than a read.
  - [1mBoth[0m — a current-user store that also appends a login event. Most capable, but it's two subsystems' worth of design,
    and I'd want a concrete need for the history before recommending it.

  [1mMy recommendation: the current-user store.[0m Everything you've described concretely — other forms needing the user — is
  a read of the present value, not a query over history. It's the smaller thing to build, and if audit history turns out
  to matter, adding an event log on top of a clean current-user interface is additive rather than a rewrite.

